{
  "root_cause": "The datepicker's keydown handler (_onKeyChange) does not include a case for the Tab key (keyCode 9). When the datepicker is visible, pressing Tab results in no explicit handling, and subsequent blur/hide logic inadvertently returns focus to the check‑in input after it briefly moves to the guests field.",
  "repair_strategy": "Add an explicit case for keyCode 9 in the _onKeyChange handler that does nothing (break). This guarantees the Tab key is processed by the handler and prevents any implicit default‑prevention behavior, allowing the browser's normal focus traversal to proceed to the next field.",
  "expected_accessibility_effect": "After typing a date in the check‑in field and pressing Tab, focus will move to the guests field and remain there, enabling keyboard‑only users to continue filling the form without unexpected focus returns.",
  "functional_risks": [
    "Adding a case for Tab could interfere with custom keyboard shortcuts if any were previously relying on the unhandled Tab key to bubble up; however, Tab is reserved for focus navigation and no such shortcuts are defined in the component."
  ],
  "verification_requirements": [
    "Verify that pressing Tab after entering a date moves focus to the guests field and does not return focus to the check‑in input.",
    "Ensure that datepicker keyboard navigation (arrow keys, Enter, Esc) continues to work as expected.",
    "Confirm that the datepicker still opens/closes correctly on field focus and blur."
  ]
}
